TEMPORARY diagnostic patch, RESTORE_CORRUPTION_PLAN.md Phase C
("checksum triangulation"). Not meant to ship -- delete once the restore-
corruption investigation (docs/kv-offload-cache-identity-corruption.md)
closes. Adds content checksums at three points in the KV-offload
restore path, gated behind VLLM_KV_RESTORE_CHECKSUM=1 (off by default,
zero cost when unset):

  (a) STORE_DONE  -- v1/kv_offload/cpu/gpu_worker.py, SingleDirection-
      OffloadingHandler.get_finished(): once a GPU->CPU store transfer's
      end_event confirms completion, crc32 each copied CPU row (one row
      = one (layer, chunk-page) unit). Metadata (tensor_idx, cpu_tensor,
      cpu_block_id, gpu_block_id) is captured at submission time in
      transfer_async() and stashed on the new Transfer.checksum_meta
      field so get_finished() can hash without re-deriving pointers.
      Deferred to completion (not submission) because the GPU->CPU copy
      is async -- hashing before end_event fires would read stale CPU
      memory, not the just-written content.

  (b) LOAD_SUBMIT -- same file, transfer_async(), CPU->GPU direction:
      crc32 each CPU row about to be read, at submission time (before
      the async copy starts, per the plan -- CPU-tier content is static
      at this point so submission-time and completion-time reads are
      equivalent, but submission is cheaper and avoids adding a second
      hook in get_finished).

  (c) RESTORE_READ -- v1/worker/mamba_utils.py, preprocess_mamba(): when
      prev_state_idx has no local mamba_state_idx history (the cold-
      resume fallback `(num_computed_tokens - 1) // block_size`, which
      fires identically for an offload restore AND an ordinary local
      prefix-cache-hit new conversation turn -- exactly the two-case
      comparison Phase C needs), crc32 the GPU state block at that index
      for every mamba layer before this step's forward pass reads it.
      Costs one .cpu() sync per layer; only runs under the debug env var.

Both (b) and (c) use the same crc32-over-logical-bytes scheme and are
both tagged by GPU block id, so LOAD_SUBMIT and RESTORE_READ rows for the
same restore join directly on (gpu_blk, crc). (a) and (b)/(STORE_DONE and
a later LOAD_SUBMIT for the same offload key) join on cpu_blk instead,
since CPU-tier block allocation is content-addressed and stable across a
store -> (idle in RAM) -> load round trip for one key, while GPU block
ids get reused across unrelated allocations over a session's lifetime and
so cannot correlate a store to a much-later load of the same content.

Also adds one symmetric debug line, LOAD_JOB_BLOCKS (distributed/kv_
transfer/kv_connector/v1/offloading/scheduler.py, update_state_after_
alloc()) -- the existing offload-verbose-eviction-debug-logging.patch
already logs STORE_JOB_BLOCKS (job -> req_id, src_block_ids) but had no
load-side equivalent; this fills that gap so a load job's job_id can be
correlated back to its req_id and offload keys the same way.

